{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for deployment note exact clean head 2e1b23cf15212c0f453f8eb84149321ca4dce18c. The four original themes are materially improved, and fetch/retention language is now honest, but three load-bearing gaps remain. F1 BLOCKER: the claim step says a CAS push moves inbox to claimed but never requires an atomic multi-ref transaction. I reproduced the failure against a local bare receive-pack using explicit force-with-lease expectations: with claimed already present, the non-atomic push rejected claimed as stale yet deleted inbox, silently losing the candidate. The same command with --atomic rejected both updates and preserved inbox. Specify one --atomic push containing claimed creation and inbox deletion, both explicit leases; require/measure server atomic capability and add the occupied-claim partial-update case. Git documents that force-with-lease is per-ref while --atomic is what makes all refs update or none: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push. F2 MUST FIX: reversible base32hex makes characters legal but does not bound component length. A 300-byte ref component passed git check-ref-format locally yet update-ref failed with File name too long; a valid 300-byte idempotency key expands to about 480 base32 characters. Bound the canonical key or use a fixed digest of the full target/namespace/actor/key tuple and verify it against the candidate; extend case 9 with boundary/oversize keys and actual ref creation, not only check-ref-format. F3 BLOCKER: the isolated control repo is correct, but repository_dispatch is not a safe submitter trigger as written. GitHub requires Contents-write permission on the control repository to create that event, contradicting the statement that submitters have no control-repo write access. Use schedule only or name a trusted GitHub App/webhook relay whose authority and executable-code boundary case 7 tests. Evidence: https://docs.github.com/en/rest/repos/repos#create-a-repository-dispatch-event. What holds